recommender toggle\nL139: \nL140: CORE Recommender (cite40†What is CORE?†core.ac.uk )\nL141: \nL142:   * Author\nL143:   * Venue\nL144:   * Institution\nL145:   * Topic\nL146: \nL147: [Input] About arXivLabs\nL148: # arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\nL149: \nL150: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL151: \nL152: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL153: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite41†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL154: \nL155: cite42†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite43†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL156: \nL157: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite44†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL158: cite45†About†info.arxiv.org · cite46†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite47†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite48†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L159: \nL160: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFlexPipe: Adapting Dynamic LLM Serving Through Inflight Pipeline Refactoring in Fragmented Serverless Clusters (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11938v1)\nciteturn2795view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11938v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 485\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract. L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Background L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Distributed LLM Inference Paradigms L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Serverless Challenges for LLM Serving L20:   4. cite10†3 Motivation L21:     1. cite11†3.1 Resource Fragmentation in Cloud L22:     2. cite12†3.2 Pipeline Granularity and Data Parallelism L23:     3. cite13†3.3 Pipeline Overhead in Request Distributions L24:   5. cite14†4 System Overview L25:   6. cite15†5 Fine-Grained Model Partitioning L26:   7. cite16†6 Inflight Pipeline Refactoring L27:     1. cite17†6.1 Granularity Adaptation L28:     2. cite18†6.2 Hardware Efficiency Optimization L29:     3. cite19†6.3 Consistency Maintenance L30:   8. cite20†7 Adaptive Pipeline Scaling L31:   9. cite21†8 Implementation L32:   10. cite22†9 Evaluation L33:     1. cite23†9.1 End-to-End Performance L34:     2. cite24†9.2 Performance Stability L35:     3. cite25†9.3 Pipeline Stall Recovery L36:     4. cite26†9.4 Resource Efficiency L37:     5. cite27†9.5 Performance in Production Workloads L38:     6. cite28†9.6 Case Study in Production Cluster L39:   11. cite29†10 Related Work L40:   12. cite30†11 Conclusion L41:   13. cite31†12 Acknowledgments L42:   14. cite32†References L43: cite33†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L44: \nL45: arXiv:2510.11938v1 [cs.DC] 13 Oct 2025\nL46: # FlexPipe: Adapting Dynamic LLM Serving\nL47: Through Inflight Pipeline Refactoring in Fragmented Serverless Clusters\nL48: CCS: Computer systems organization Distributed architectures CCS: Computer systems organization Cloud computing CCS: Computing methodologies Distributed artificial intelligence Conference: European Conference on Computer Systems; April 27–30, 2026; Edinburgh, Scotland Uk European Conference on Computer Systems (EUROSYS ’26), April 27–30, 2026, Edinburgh, Scotland Uk DOI: cite34†10.1145/3767295.3769316†doi.org ISBN: 979-8-4007-2212-7/26/04\nL49: Yanying Lin Affiliation: Shenzhen Institutes of Advanced Technology, CAS; UCAS Affiliation: UC San Diego , Shijie Peng Affiliation: Shenzhen Institutes of Advanced Technology, CAS Affiliation: UCAS , Chengzhi Lu Affiliation: Nanyang Technological University , Chengzhong Xu Affiliation: University of Macau and Kejiang Ye^{*} Note: Corresponding Author Affiliation: Shenzhen Institutes of Advanced Technology, CAS\nL50: \nL51: © cc\nL52: ###### Abstract.\nL53: \nL54: Serving Large Language Models (LLMs) in production faces significant challenges from highly variable request patterns and severe resource fragmentation in serverless clusters. Current systems rely on static pipeline configurations that struggle to adapt to dynamic workload conditions, leading to substantial inefficiencies.\nL55: We present FlexPipe, a novel system that dynamically reconfigures pipeline architectures during runtime to address these fundamental limitations.\nL56: FlexPipe decomposes models into fine-grained stages and intelligently adjusts pipeline granularity based on real-time request pattern analysis, implementing three key innovations: fine-grained model partitioning with preserved computational graph constraints, inflight pipeline refactoring with consistent cache transitions, and topology-aware resource allocation that navigates GPU fragmentation.\nL57: Comprehensive evaluation on an 82-GPU cluster demonstrates that FlexPipe achieves up to 8.5× better resource efficiency while maintaining 38.3% lower latency compared to state-of-the-art systems, reducing GPU reservation requirements from 75% to 30% of peak capacity.\nL58: ###### Keywords:\nL59: \nL60: Large Language Models, Serverless Computing, Resource Efficiency, Pipeline Parallelism, Dynamic Scaling\nL61: \nL62: ^{†}^{†}cc-license: by\nL63: ## 1. Introduction\nL64: The exponential growth in Large Language Model (LLM) parameters (cite35†Touvron et al., 2 27 ; cite36†Touvron et al., 7 19 ; cite37†Brown et al., 2020 ; cite38†Devlin et al., 5 24 ; cite39†Anil et al., 9 13 ) has created significant challenges for serving these models in production environments.\nL65: With models scaling to hundreds of billions of parameters, deploying efficient inference systems requires distributed computing approaches as single-device execution becomes infeasible due to memory constraints (cite40†Aminabadi et al., 2 11 ; cite41†Narayanan et al., 0 27 ; cite42†Zheng et al., 2022 ).\nL66: Current serving systems primarily rely on two distributed paradigms: tensor parallelism, which distributes matrix operations across devices with high-bandwidth interconnects, and pipeline parallelism, which segments models into sequential stages with lower communication requirements (cite43†Huang et al., 2 08 ; cite44†Li et al., 2023 ).\nL67: However, these approaches face substantial challenges when deployed in real-world serverless environments (cite45†Yang et al., 2 28 ; cite46†Lin et al., 4 07 ), particularly in balancing computational efficiency with resource adaptability under dynamic request patterns (cite47†Zhang et al., 2023 ) and fragmented cluster resources (cite48†Weng et al., 2023 ).\nL68: Production analysis of Alibaba clusters ^{1}^{1} 1 Request distribution traces and GPU data are openly available at cite49†https://github.com/alibaba/clusterdata/tree/master/cluster-trace-v2026-GenAI†github.com . reveals two fundamental challenges that current LLM serving systems cannot address. i) workload volatility: request patterns exhibit extreme variability with coefficient of variation (CV) fluctuating up to 7× across timeframes (Fig.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.115